A warning nobody can hear is not a check

CONFIG_DEBUG_PRINTF is off in every build here, because a sandboxed FSKit
extension has no stdout to print to. ext4_dbg therefore compiled to
nothing, and with it went every DBG_WARN in the tree -- including
twenty-two "checksum failed" sites across seven files. The volumes paid to
compute those checksums and nobody ever learned the answer.

The two loud levels now reach the shim's logger, the same arrangement
ext4b_assert_fail already uses and for the same reason. Level is a string
prefix rather than part of the mask, so the filtering is done on the
format string; DBG_INFO and unprefixed tracing stay silent, being
per-block and enough to bury a log.

Turning them on immediately proved the point, in the other direction.
Twenty-two warnings appeared in one run of the write suite, all identical
and all false: "Extent block checksum failed. Blocknr: 0". Block 0 is the
extent header living in the inode's own i_block, where sixty bytes hold
four extents and nothing else -- find_ext4_extent_tail() then points just
past them, at whatever the inode has there, and the comparison is against
noise. The root's integrity comes from the inode checksum, which covers it
already. Only a real extent block carries a tail, so only a real block is
checked now.

Both halves matter and neither works alone. A check whose failure is
invisible is not a check; a check that cries wolf on every extent-mapped
file is worse, because the first true report reads as more of the same.
With the root exempted, every suite runs silent -- write, bounds, format,
preallocation, orphan, all zero -- and a cell in each of the offline and
mounted suites now asserts that silence, so the next warning to appear
means something.

